WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:01.770
(pensive music)

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.360
- [Narrator] The Tokyo
Olympics made history

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.120
even before they began.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.140
They're the first Games
to ever be postponed.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:09.090
They're also on track

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:12.570
to be the most expensive
Summer Olympics Games ever.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.000
- So when Japan won its bid

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.730
to host the Olympics in 2013,

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.620
it said that it would
cost around $7.4 billion

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.800
but before the pandemic,

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.800
the budget had risen
by 70% to $12.6 billion

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.724
and that's not really surprising.

00:00:33.724 --> 00:00:35.530
- [Narrator] Later,
Japan's Board of Audit said

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.800
the cost would exceed $20 billion.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.613
That was before
pandemic-related costs set in.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.880
- So the biggest costs for the Olympics

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.520
are the facilities.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.280
The new National Stadium here in Tokyo,

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.860
as well as several other large facilities,

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.965
aquatic center and others
that have been built

00:00:53.965 --> 00:00:55.700
for the Games.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.523
And those cost over $3 billion.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.090
And there was about the same amount

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.960
that was spent on
renovating other facilities

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.186
to bring them up to
standard for the Games.

00:01:06.186 --> 00:01:09.560
So the whole thing, in terms of venues,

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.610
was around $7.5 billion.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.120
And the other big cost
is the operational budget

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.150
for the Olympics.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.513
It's around $2 billion.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.350
- [Narrator] Almost every Olympics ends up

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.410
going over budget.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.240
Bids are usually an optimistic estimate.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.060
- What's different in Tokyo, of course,

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.130
is that we've had this one-year delay

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.470
and we've also had the pandemic.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.270
So the one-year postponement meant

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.650
that there were additional costs incurred

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.880
for having to renegotiate contracts

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.310
for venues, for having to keep staff on

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.470
for an additional period

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:43.540
and various other things,

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.313
which added to the overall budget.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.430
- [Narrator] And the public
risk from the pandemic

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:50.480
hasn't gone away,

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.330
so the Tokyo Olympics are now proceeding

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:53.510
with COVID countermeasures

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.040
that require new costs,
manpower and infrastructure.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.270
Together, the postponement
and COVID measures

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.010
will add an estimated $2.5 billion

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:05.690
to the Games' budget
according to event organizers.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:07.080
On top of those direct costs,

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.520
COVID is also taking away much

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.970
of Japan's ability to
recoup what they've spent.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:14.320
- Ticket sales would
be around $800 million

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.810
and now they'll be close to zero

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:19.970
because there'll be almost no spectators.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.190
And there'll be almost
no income from visitors.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.600
Of course, athletes and officials

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.010
who have come here from overseas

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.280
might spend some money

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.550
but nothing like the amounts

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.330
that the organizers were hoping for

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:36.190
from spectators who
would fill the stadiums,

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:40.420
buy merchandise, spend money
in hotels and restaurants

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.620
and that could be, some economists say,

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.944
as much as $2 billion and now that's lost.

00:02:46.944 --> 00:02:48.190
(lively music)

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.110
- [Narrator] Corporate sponsors

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.070
have contributed around
$3 billion to the Games

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:55.310
and the IOC has contributed $650 million

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.670
to help cover the extra costs
resulting from the pandemic.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.350
Still, that leaves the
bulk of the costs to Japan

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.360
with little ability to
recoup in the short term.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.370
But even the most
expensive Summer Olympics

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:07.990
in history pales in comparison

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:10.150
to the overall cost of the pandemic.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.930
- Japan has spent around $800 billon

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:17.940
on stimulus packages, three of them,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.800
because of COVID.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.120
The pandemic is obviously
the most expensive thing

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:23.980
to deal with here.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:25.290
It's not the Olympics.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.360
But if the Olympics
makes the pandemic worse

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.367
and there has to be new stimulus measures,

00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:33.680
those could be fair larger

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.813
than the actual budget of the Olympics.

